#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/id #uuid "ec1aaa71-efe4-50fd-8552-bcc98abbd3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-218" :question/problem "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} .\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min},$ so we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(-2)\\cdot 2(10)=-40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/id #uuid "8780fe5c-94c5-5ac6-b6f6-6e86df29b416" :question/math-academy-id "e-219" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{ cm}^2\\text{/s}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds." :question/worked-solution "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\text{ cm[math]^2[/math]/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "6264adc4-c5ab-5b84-b89f-27b4382baa0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-220" :question/problem "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{ mm}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "7187c402-9949-5671-af33-8929bc5344a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-221" :question/problem "The sides of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to twice the rate of increase of the diagonal. What is the area of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efd2b787-6391-5191-895f-2787cebf3d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8mm^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061898} {:answer/id #uuid "1e261b49-bedf-501d-b314-7a3a87bd2175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144mm^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061899} {:answer/id #uuid "67941803-cd90-5a7f-a42b-abfb92c3b8e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24mm^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061900} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1bdc23-9638-5bad-9d54-ca42678baf1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36mm^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061901} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe15eb2-3697-5cfc-9463-cec964358879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72mm^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061902}] :answer-field/id #uuid "eef0677f-b6e1-5448-a282-0aa8c9254820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061897}] :question/id #uuid "3d9b3637-d8c0-5460-ba5e-d5e1eb8257d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-24800" :question/problem "![](images/74/740d181b8e8eff44f0eb258c8b68f30d05f70445aefabd5c00fd68e570e825dd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $3mm/s$. At what rate is the area of the square changing when the sides have a length of $12mm$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542} :answer-field/id #uuid "6262049f-56fe-5753-8d3a-24cb4987048f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d06153ec-06de-5e08-a5e0-36bc3ef9e7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-314495" :question/problem "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to seven times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length $h$ of the diagonal of the square at that moment?\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 7\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 7 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $7\\,\\text{ ft}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a74594c-0ef1-5fc3-9a5b-718328e0c736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a74594c-0ef1-5fc3-9a5b-718328e0c736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372892} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe1680e-65fa-58d0-9937-619069d11c05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "326e805a-53b2-5770-bd3f-5b9abe674ca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344438" :question/problem "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to twelve times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{ m}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 12 & =2x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=6\\text{ m}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={6}^{2}=36{\\text{ m}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6ddcc7-548b-528c-b8e3-9ff38fb06abf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "175199e1-96a5-594b-b670-66eb928d5ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344507" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $30{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a4/a42b7ac5c9f5870d256b6474c9b95730d32184ba22ca80235c76ffe0ff346f31.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\text{ }{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{15}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500052} :answer-field/id #uuid "540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" :question/math-academy-id "q-344664" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797a4f0c-845d-5bd4-a00e-f4042c09b16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392644} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bd86db-1af1-529e-b7e3-3d8c2f9d1330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392645} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff2b416-758c-5d4f-944b-1eac169b1cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392646} {:answer/id #uuid "bb340c74-6d19-51d7-b6b4-c00f66ac9476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392647} {:answer/id #uuid "71d4db78-dc17-5a6a-ab36-7c6870d8b79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb340c74-6d19-51d7-b6b4-c00f66ac9476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392647} :answer-field/id #uuid "50391d24-edeb-556b-9cad-499093215c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7963534b-05e1-5074-b217-dce61952496a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48381" :question/problem "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to four times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length of the diagonals of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 4 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $4\\text{ feet}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0637a4d0-6b43-5e08-9f4b-cf24e433081c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373190} {:answer/id #uuid "5c81e1aa-915b-5422-99f0-467b0e070289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373191} {:answer/id #uuid "815fc749-b444-53da-9fc2-2e5989eb4ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373192} {:answer/id #uuid "b8909279-d59c-52c0-9000-cf5c167053c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373193} {:answer/id #uuid "696bc3f8-d7cc-5732-ae41-e24bf7b3365b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c81e1aa-915b-5422-99f0-467b0e070289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373191} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8b8ebc5-b1cb-51e5-ace6-4230ff06b936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6f517ab-ab71-54a6-88e9-bc765cc4550f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73318" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in minutes." :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-12\\text{ in}{}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{6}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
